PATCH 513: the 209e dirty-walk dispatched its handlers with AX read from UNINITIALISED STACK.

This is the native<->wasm parity carrier.  Measured on AZER4: the two targets are bit-identical through
t=292 and diverge at t=293 in DGROUP block 0 at exactly ONE byte -- DGROUP:0x9004 goes 0x68 -> 3 on
native and stays 0x68 on wasm.  3 is the display-list cell's "dirty" mark, written by the walk's input
handlers as `movb [bp+si],3`, and they only reach it when `test ax,0x80` is true and `test ax,1` false.

The asm keeps AX live all the way in:
    459a/45f7: ... call 0x4691 ; call 0x206f          ; AX = the event/poll word
    206f:      mov bx,[0x3e04] ; mov dx,[0x3e06] ; mov bp,[0x3afe] ; call 0x209e   ; AX untouched
    209e:      mov di,[bx+2] ; mov al,(bp,di) ; mov cl,[0x73d] ; shr al,cl ; mov (bp,di),al
               jae .. ; mov [0x3e08],bx ; mov di,[bx] ; call *0x423c(di)
so at `20b6 call *0x423c(%di)` AX = (AH from 206f's caller) : (AL = the cell byte AFTER the shift).  The
handlers test AL bits 0/2/4/7 and AH bit 2 (`test ax,0x400`).

Two defects.  Ghidra typed 206f's parameter `undefined1`, throwing the high byte away, and the three
call sites narrowed with `(char)`.  And the port's dirty-walk dispatch was ARG-LESS, so every handler's
`param_1` was whatever happened to be on the stack -- genuinely undefined behaviour, and therefore a
different value under gcc -m32 than under emcc.  That is exactly the class of divergence the
native<->wasm invariant exists to catch, and it is why the AI-combat cascade (patch 494 onward) broke
parity in several missions: it made far more of this walk reachable.

Fixed at the source: 206f publishes g_fist_evax (the same mechanism patch 142 uses for the walk base BP),
209e composes g_fist_paintax = (g_fist_evax & 0xff00) | <shifted cell byte> immediately before the
dispatch AND passes it as the dispatch argument, so every handler -- the ten patch-504/509 input
handlers explicitly, and all the others through their own `param_1` -- now sees the register the asm
actually has in flight instead of stack garbage.
